1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:04,720
Rysh is a terminal multiplexer
built for coding agents.

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:09,360
One Go binary, and you install it the
usual way. That's the open source build,

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:15,182
straight from the public module,
and it lands on your path as rysh.

4
00:00:17,805 --> 00:00:22,525
Start a session by name. Rysh
runs it in a background daemon,

5
00:00:22,525 --> 00:00:25,565
which is the detail everything
else in this video rests on.

6
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,740
The session is not your terminal. Your
terminal is just a window onto it.

7
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:36,300
Inside a session, work is organised
in tabs. New tab, and you get one.

8
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:39,740
Name it whatever the work is called, and
the name is what you'll navigate by later.

9
00:00:42,375 --> 00:00:46,695
A tab splits into lanes. A lane
is a vertical column of the tab,

10
00:00:46,695 --> 00:00:49,175
and it's the unit you
group related work into.

11
00:00:49,655 --> 00:00:51,575
Here's a second one, next to the first.

12
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:55,510
Inside a lane, panes stack.

13
00:00:55,590 --> 00:00:59,110
New stack, two, and now there
are three panes in this lane,

14
00:00:59,110 --> 00:01:01,830
one on top of the other,
each with its own shell.

15
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:04,630
Stacking is how you keep several things open

16
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:06,310
without shrinking any of them to nothing.

17
00:01:08,315 --> 00:01:09,915
And now the point of all of it.

18
00:01:09,915 --> 00:01:13,275
Type hash hash claude in a
pane, and Claude Code starts

19
00:01:13,275 --> 00:01:16,795
right there, with its
conversation pinned to that pane.

20
00:01:17,195 --> 00:01:21,733
Control S and a number moves down the stack,
so the second pane gets its own Claude

21
00:01:21,733 --> 00:01:26,400
— a separate process, a separate
conversation, nothing shared between them.

22
00:01:42,495 --> 00:01:46,815
Once more for the third. Then
back to the top of the stack,

23
00:01:46,890 --> 00:01:50,250
where the first one is still
sitting exactly as you left it.

24
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:53,770
Three panes, three
independent Claude sessions,

25
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:56,970
all running at the same time in
a single terminal window — and

26
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:00,490
none of them tied to this terminal,
which is what the next video is about.

